\name{BaseRTermplotLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRTermplotLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Term Plot Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{termplot()} as the partial-effect curves it draws: one panel per
term, the term's contribution to the fit plotted against its own carrier.

\strong{A grid, not a layer.} Like \code{pairs()} and \code{lag.plot()}, one \code{termplot()}
call draws several panels, and the orchestrator's ordinary multipanel path
cannot split them: \code{combine_layer_results()} maps a layer to a cell by its
\emph{group} index, and one recorded call is one group, so every curve would
land in the same cell. So this answers \code{multi_panel = TRUE} and places each
curve at its own cell.

\strong{The panels are pages, not terms.} \code{termplot()} sets no layout of its
own -- unlike \code{pairs()}, which calls \code{par(mfrow)} itself -- so the caller's
\code{par(mfrow)} decides how many terms share a page, and R starts a new page
when it runs out of cells. Only the last page is exported. Measured on a
three-term \code{lm}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{no mfrow      (k = 1)   graphics-plot-1                 1 panel
mfrow c(1,2)  (k = 2)   graphics-plot-1                 1 panel
mfrow c(1,3)  (k = 3)   graphics-plot-1 .. -3           3 panels
mfrow c(2,2)  (k = 4)   graphics-plot-1 .. -3           3 panels
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

So with \code{n} terms and \code{k} cells the page carries the \strong{last}
\code{((n - 1) \%\% k) + 1} of them, which is the rule
\code{\link[=compute_panel_slots]{compute_panel_slots()}} already applies to whole plot groups -- the same
arithmetic, one level down. A reading that announced all \code{n} terms would
name curves that are not on the page.

The \code{par} call is recorded as LAYOUT rather than as a layer, so it does not
reach the processor with the rest of the call. It is read off the device
the call was recorded on, through the same \code{\link[=detect_panel_configuration]{detect_panel_configuration()}}
the orchestrator uses, so the two cannot disagree about the grid.

\strong{What a panel draws.} The curve is the term's fitted contribution,
\code{predict(model, type = "terms")[, term]}, against that term's carrier from
the model frame, in increasing carrier order -- which is the order
\code{termplot()} sorts them into before drawing. With
\code{partial.resid = TRUE} it adds the partial residuals,
\code{contribution + residuals(model)}, as points beside the curve; measured,
that is a second grob in the same panel:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{termplot(fit)                     panel k: lines-1
termplot(fit, partial.resid = T)  panel k: lines-1 and points-1
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The points are left for a follow-up rather than emitted as a second layer:
they are a different reading of the same panel, and the curve is the thing
\code{termplot()} exists to draw.

\strong{A factor term is declined.} \code{termplot()} draws it as a step function
over the levels, which is neither this line nor a bar, and reading it as a
line would announce a slope between levels that have no order. It is left
out of the grid rather than given a wrong shape.
}
